/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  dspmde01_water_treatment_salts -- THE RESEARCHER'S PATH, PROVED: a water
  that carries nitrate, fluoride, bromide, carbonate and ammonium, fed as
  SALTS from the standard catalogue, through the DSPM-DE pore (DEV.md 4c
  C157/C161/C170).

  WHAT KIND OF CHECK THIS IS.  A STRUCTURAL witness, not a validation.  It
  shows that every one of these salts decomposes into its ions and that
  every ion finds a CITED hydrated radius (Nightingale 1959, Table I) and a
  CITED D0 (USGS PHREEQC phreeqc.dat, `-dw`) on its species record, so the
  pore can be solved.  No number here is a measurement.  The two pores are
  NanoFiltran's worked example (constant/assets/NanoFiltranExample.dat,
  which describes no real membrane) and the catalogue's NF270_dspmde (an
  archetype whose pore is set, not fitted to this water): a researcher
  replaces them with the record his own fit writes
  (tutorials/steady/membranes/membrane24_fit_then_simulate is the
  template).

  WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY.  Bicarbonate (HCO3-), phosphate and the
  hydrogen phosphates have a PHREEQC D0 but NO hydrated radius: Nightingale's
  Table I does not list them, and a Stokes radius from D0 is a different
  convention that would shift every steric hindrance in silence -- so DSPM-DE
  REFUSES them by name (DEV.md 4c C161).  NaHCO3 in this feed would stop the
  run with that refusal.  The carbonate below is the CO3-- of Na2CO3 taken
  fully dissociated: nothing here speciates it, so it stands for a high-pH
  water; at a natural pH most of it would be bicarbonate.

  THE THREE OPERATIONS, each a `poreTransport` bench at an IMPOSED J_v, ideal
  activities, NanoFiltran's "no polarisation" film (k = 10 m/s), the solved
  pore at 801 nodes:
    monovalentWater  on NANOFILTRAN'S WORKED-EXAMPLE pore (X -30 mol/m3,
                     r_p 0.40 nm, 2.5 um, eps_p 60): NaCl 5, NaNO3 1,
                     NaF 0.5, KBr 0.5, NH4Cl 0.5 mol/m3 (Na K NH4 Cl NO3 F Br)
    mixedWater       the same water plus Na2CO3 0.5 mol/m3 (+ CO3--), on the
                     catalogue's NF270_dspmde pore (r_p 0.60 nm, X -5 mol/m3)
    hardWater        Ca(NO3)2 2, KNO3 2, MgCl2 1, NaBr 1, MgSO4 1 mol/m3
                     (Ca Mg K Na NO3 Cl Br SO4), on NF270_dspmde
  WHY TWO PORES.  NanoFiltran's example pore was set against NanoFiltran's
  own ion database, whose radii are STOKES radii (Na+ 1.84, Cl- 1.21,
  SO4-- 2.31 A; nanofiltran_geraldesAlves2008/constant/species/).  Against
  the catalogue's HYDRATED radii a 0.40 nm pore nearly closes to the
  divalent ions: lambda = r/r_p is 0.985 for CO3--, 0.95 for SO4--, 1.03 for
  Ca++ and 1.07 for Mg++.  Measured 2026-10-26: Na2CO3 (alone, or in this
  water) and MgSO4 + NaCl do NOT converge there (the solved pore refuses,
  naming the stalled continuation), and MgCl2 or Ca(NO3)2 alone are refused
  because no cation enters the pore.  So the divalent waters run on
  NF270_dspmde, whose 0.60 nm pore is the one its record states is
  consistent with the hydrated-radius tier.  A pore and its ion radii are
  ONE convention: a pore fitted on one cannot be read with the other.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name      monovalentWater;
        type      poreTransport;
        T         298.15 K;
        membrane  NanoFiltranExample;
        transport DSPM-DE;
        transportParameters
        {
            poreSolver { method finiteDifference; nodes 801; wallClosure porePermeate; }
            hindrance  DechadilokDeen2006;
            activity   ideal;
            bulkDielectric 80;
        }
        feedMolPerM3 { NaCl 5; NaNO3 1; NaF 0.5; KBr 0.5; NH4Cl 0.5; }
        J_v       ( 1e-6 5e-6 2e-5 );
        k_film    10;                          // NanoFiltran's "no polarisation"
        polarisation { suctionCorrection GeraldesAfonso2006; ionCoupling electroneutral; }
    }
    {
        name      mixedWater;
        type      poreTransport;
        T         298.15 K;
        membrane  NF270_dspmde;
        transport DSPM-DE;
        transportParameters
        {
            poreSolver { method finiteDifference; nodes 801; wallClosure porePermeate; }
            hindrance  DechadilokDeen2006;
            activity   ideal;
            bulkDielectric 80;
        }
        feedMolPerM3 { NaCl 5; NaNO3 1; NaF 0.5; KBr 0.5; Na2CO3 0.5; NH4Cl 0.5; }
        J_v       ( 1e-6 5e-6 2e-5 );
        k_film    10;                          // NanoFiltran's "no polarisation"
        polarisation { suctionCorrection GeraldesAfonso2006; ionCoupling electroneutral; }
    }
    {
        name      hardWater;
        type      poreTransport;
        T         298.15 K;
        membrane  NF270_dspmde;
        transport DSPM-DE;
        transportParameters
        {
            poreSolver { method finiteDifference; nodes 801; wallClosure porePermeate; }
            hindrance  DechadilokDeen2006;
            activity   ideal;
            bulkDielectric 80;
        }
        feedMolPerM3 { calciumNitrate 2; KNO3 2; MgCl2 1; NaBr 1; MgSO4 1; }
        J_v       ( 1e-6 5e-6 2e-5 );
        k_film    10;                          // NanoFiltran's "no polarisation"
        polarisation { suctionCorrection GeraldesAfonso2006; ionCoupling electroneutral; }
    }
);
